{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b91abd6946f63530","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\n201\tHowever, a fundamental limitation of conventional DPO approaches is their offline nature. While standard DPO can achieve RLHF-like effects, it remains an offline reinforcement learning method where the implicit “reward model” remains static throughout training. This limitation creates a critical distribution shift problem: as the policy model improves, it increasingly diverges from the preference model’s distribution, ultimately leading to performance bottlenecks. Our work addresses this limitation by introducing the first online DPO methodology for video-to-audio (V2A) generation. Our approach achieves the adaptive benefits of online reinforcement learning while preserving the computational efficiency of DPO. This innovation is particularly valuable in the V2A domain, where no prior alignment techniques have been applied, and where the complex, context-dependent nature of audio generation demands more dynamic preference modeling.\n202\t\n203\t# 3 Method\n204\t\n205\tIn this section, we begin by introducing the foundational concepts of LDMs and their applications in audio generation. We then present our proposed method, HarmoniDPO (HDPO), which integrates video, image to enable cross-modal audio generation with enhanced performance.\n206\t\n207\t# 3.1 Preliminaries\n208\t\n209\tLatent diffusion models (LDMs) 41 are a class of generative models built upon the principles of Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) 19. LDMs have emerged as powerful frameworks in text-to-audio and video-to-audio generation tasks. A key feature of LDMs is that they operate in a lower-dimensional latent space, making them computationally efficient. In the context of audio, raw signals are first transformed into mel-spectrograms. The mel-spectrogram, a time-frequency representation, logarithmically scales its frequency axis to the Mel scale, which mirrors human auditory perception, enabling the model to effectively learn both temporal dynamics and spectral characteristics.\n210\t\n211\tIn a video-to-audio system, given an audio signal xax_{a} and its corresponding video xvx_{v}, we first use a pre-trained variational autoencoder (VAE) with an encoder ℰθ\\mathcal{E}_{\\theta} to compress the mel-spectrogram representation of xax_{a} into a low-dimensional latent representation, denoted as z0=ℰθ​(xa)z_{0}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\theta}(x_{a}). This latent vector z0z_{0} is the target for the diffusion model.\n212\t\n213\tThe forward diffusion process gradually adds Gaussian noise to the latent vector z0z_{0} over a sequence of TT timesteps. This process is a fixed Markov chain defined as:\n214\t\n215\tq⁡(zt|zt−1)=𝒩⁡(zt,1−βt​zt−1,βt​𝐈)q(z_{t}|z_{t-1})=\\mathcal{N}(z_{t};\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{t}}z_{t-1},\\beta_{t}\\mathbf{I})\n216\t\n217\t(1)\n218\t\n219\twhere {βt}t=1T\\{\\beta_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} is a pre-defined variance schedule. A useful property of this process is that we can sample ztz_{t} directly from z0z_{0} at any timestep tt:\n220\t\n221\tq⁡(zt|z0)=𝒩⁡(zt,α¯t​z0,(1−α¯t)​𝐈)q(z_{t}|z_{0})=\\mathcal{N}(z_{t};\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}z_{0},(1-\\bar{\\alpha}_{t})\\mathbf{I})\n222\t\n223\t(2)\n224\t\n225\twhere α¯t=∏s=1t(1−βs)\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}=\\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\\beta_{s}). Using the reparameterization trick, any ztz_{t} can be expressed as zt=α¯t​z0+1−α¯t​ϵz_{t}=\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}z_{0}+\\sqrt{1-\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}\\epsilon, where ϵ∼𝒩⁡(0,𝐈)\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\mathbf{I}).\n226\t\n227\tThe reverse process aims to learn the true posterior q⁡(zt−1|zt)q(z_{t-1}|z_{t}), which would reverse the noising process. As this posterior is intractable, a neural network ϵθ\\epsilon_{\\theta} is trained to approximate it. As shown by Ho et al. 19, instead of modeling the reverse distribution’s mean directly, the training can be simplified to predicting the noise component ϵ\\epsilon that was added to create ztz_{t}. This leads to a simplified training objective, which is a mean-squared error loss between the true and predicted noise:\n228\t\n229\tℒLDM=𝔼z0,ϵ,t​[‖ϵ−ϵθ​(zt,t)‖22]\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{LDM}}=\\mathbb{E}_{z_{0},\\epsilon,t}\\left[\\|\\epsilon-\\epsilon_{\\theta}(z_{t},t)\\|_{2}^{2}\\right]\n230\t\n231\t(3)\n232\t\n233\tTo enable conditional generation, the denoising network ϵθ\\epsilon_{\\theta} is augmented with conditioning information, such as a visual embedding fvf_{v}, typically via a cross-attention mechanism. The objective thus becomes:\n234\t\n235\tℒLDM=𝔼z0,ϵ,t,fv​[‖ϵ−ϵθ​(zt,t,fv)‖22]\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{LDM}}=\\mathbb{E}_{z_{0},\\epsilon,t,f_{v}}\\left[\\|\\epsilon-\\epsilon_{\\theta}(z_{t},t,f_{v})\\|_{2}^{2}\\right]\n236\t\n237\t(4)\n238\t\n239\tDuring inference, given a visual embedding fvf_{v}, we can generate the corresponding audio by iterative denoising from random Gaussian noise zTz_{T}:\n240\t\n241\tzt−1=11−βt​(zt−βt1−α¯​t​ϵθ​(zt,t,fv))z_{t-1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{t}}}(z_{t}-\\frac{\\beta_{t}}{\\sqrt{1-\\bar{\\alpha}t}}\\epsilon_{\\theta}(z_{t},t,f_{v}))\n242\t\n243\t(5)\n244\t\n245\tGiven the limited size and varying quality of existing visual-audio datasets, we use a pre-trained text-to-audio diffusion model, Tango-2 35, as our base model, keeping it frozen throughout training. Figure 2 provides an overview of the framework, and we will describe each component of HarmoniDPO in detail in the following sections.\n246\t\n247\tFigure 2: Illustration of our HarmoniDPO model architecture. We propose a dual-feature representation framework that combines global video features with fine-grained frame-wise features.\n248\t\n249\t# 3.2 Visual Condition\n250\t\n251\tAccurate video representation is essential for effectively capturing the semantic content of videos. Previous methods, such as FoleyCrafter, have relied on the CLIP image encoder to obtain average semantic features from individual frames. However, since CLIP is not trained on video datasets, this approach may fall short of capturing the temporal dynamics and motion patterns inherent in video content. To address this limitation, we propose a dual-feature representation framework that combines global video features with fine-grained frame-wise features, enabling comprehensive encoding of both spatial and temporal information.\n252\t\n253\tAs shown in Figure  2, for global video feature extraction, we leverage the video encoder from InternVid 53, a state-of-the-art contrastive video-language pre-training framework. Given an input video VV containing TT frames, the global video feature fgf_{g} is computed as:\n254\t\n255\tfg=ℰInternVid​(V)∈ℝdgf_{g}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{InternVid}}(V)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{g}}\n256\t\n257\t(6)\n258\t\n259\twhere ℰInternVid\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{InternVid}} represents the InternVid video encoder and dgd_{g} denotes the dimensionality of the extracted video feature.\n260\tInternVid’s architecture extends CLIP’s visual understanding capabilities through continued pre-training on large-scale video-text datasets, enabling superior modeling of temporal relationships and motion dynamics across video sequences. The encoding process operates as follows: given a target video, we first establish a fixed frame sampling count MM. The video is then temporally subsampled at MM uniformly distributed timestamps to ensure consistent temporal coverage. The resulting frames are concatenated and processed through a pre-trained Vision Transformer to produce the final global video representation.\n261\t\n262\tWhile the global video features effectively capture high-level semantic content and temporal dynamics, fine-grained frame-level details remain crucial for maintaining precise temporal alignment between the generated audio and specific visual events. Therefore, we implement a complementary frame-level processing stream that operates parallel to global feature extraction. We uniformly sample NN frames {v1,v2,…,vN}\\{v_{1},v_{2},\\dots,v_{N}\\} from the input video and process each frame through the CLIP visual encoder to obtain frame-wise embeddings:\n263\t\n264\tei=ℰCLIP(vi)∈ℝdf,i∈1,2,…,Ne_{i}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{CLIP}}(v_{i})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{f}},\\quad i\\in{1,2,\\dots,N}\n265\t\n266\t(7)\n267\t\n268\twhere ℰCLIP\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{CLIP}} represents the CLIP image encoder and dfd_{f} is the dimension of frame features. In our implementation, we sample N=250N=250 frames from the original video as uniformly as possible. For videos containing fewer than 250 frames, we pad the sequence with zero-filled frame features. Note that the majority of samples in our training dataset exceed 250 frames, making padding necessary for only a small portion of the data.\n269\t\n270\tFor effective multimodal fusion, we first use projection layers to align the dimensions of global and frame-level features to a common dimension dcd_{c}:\n271\t\n272\tfg′\\displaystyle f^{\\prime}_{g}\n273\t=Wg​fg∈ℝdc\\displaystyle=W_{g}f_{g}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}\n274\t\n275\t(8)\n276\t\n277\tei′\\displaystyle e^{\\prime}_{i}\n278\t=Wf​ei∈ℝdc\\displaystyle=W_{f}e_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}\n279\t\n280\t(9)\n281\t\n282\twhere Wg∈ℝdc×dgW_{g}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}\\times d_{g}} and Wf∈ℝdc×dfW_{f}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}\\times d_{f}} are learnable projection matrices. We then concatenate the projected global video feature with the projected frame-wise embeddings:\n283\t\n284\tF=[fg′;e1′;e2′;…;eN′]∈ℝ(1+N)×dcF=[f^{\\prime}_{g};e^{\\prime}_{1};e^{\\prime}_{2};\\dots;e^{\\prime}_{N}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{c}}\n285\t\n286\t(10)\n287\t\n288\tFinally, we employ another projection layer to transform the concatenated features to the model’s hidden dimension:\n289\t\n290\tF′=Wh​F∈ℝ(1+N)×dhF^{\\prime}=W_{h}F\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}}\n291\t\n292\t(11)\n293\t\n294\twhere Wh∈ℝdh×dcW_{h}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\\times d_{c}} is the final projection matrix and dhd_{h} is the hidden dimension of the model.\n295\t\n296\tTo better capture temporal relationships among the fused features, we incorporate Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) 43 within a multi-head self-attention mechanism. The self-attention operation employs separate learned projections for queries, keys, and values:\n297\t\n298\tQ=F′​Wq∈ℝ(1+N)×dh,K=F′​Wk∈ℝ(1+N)×dh,V=F′​Wv∈ℝ(1+N)×dh\\displaystyle Q=F^{\\prime}W_{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}},\\ K=F^{\\prime}W_{k}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}},\\ V=F^{\\prime}W_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}}\n299\t\n300\t(12)\n301\t\n302\twhere Wq,Wk,Wv∈ℝdh×dhW_{q},W_{k},W_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\\times d_{h}} are learnable projection matrices for queries, keys, and values, respectively. The final fused representation is obtained by:\n303\t\n304\tFfused=AttentionRoPE​(Q,K,V)∈ℝ(1+N)×dhF_{\\text{fused}}=\\text{Attention}_{\\text{RoPE}}(Q,K,V)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}}\n305\t\n306\t(13)\n307\t\n308\twhere RoPE is applied to both queries QQ and keys KK before computing attention scores, enabling the model to encode sequential ordering of frames through relative position information. These fused representations are subsequently incorporated into the diffusion model via a cross-attention layer (visual-attention), enabling the denoising process to dynamically attend to spatio-temporal features during training. \n309\t\n310\tBuilding upon the comprehensive visual, our method integrates these multimodal conditions into a diffusion-based audio generation framework. As illustrated in Figure  3, the generation process operates within the latent space of a pre-trained Variational Autoencoder (VAE) for computational efficiency. The reverse diffusion process begins with noisy latent variables zTz_{T} and iteratively denoises them through a U-Net architecture, where the fused visual representations FfusedF_{\\text{fused}} are injected via cross-attention layers to guide the denoising process. The final denoised latent z0z_{0} is then decoded through the VAE decoder to generate the target audio output.\n311\t\n312\tOur model also supports the optional textual conditioning for enhanced control over the generation process. For the Text input, we use the pre-trained FLAN-T56 text encoder to extract the text embedding ftf_{t}. Given a text prompt TT, the text embedding ftf_{t} is computed as:\n313\t\n314\tft=ℰT5text​(T)∈ℝdtf_{t}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{T5}}^{\\text{text}}(T)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}}\n315\t\n316\t(14)\n317\t\n318\twhere ℰT5text\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{T5}}^{\\text{text}} denotes the FLAN-T5 text encoder and dtd_{t} is the dimension of the text feature. We use a projection layer to match the hidden dimension. The diffusion model then integrates these textual conditions through the cross-attention layer.\n319\t\n320\tFigure 3: Overview of the video-conditioned audio generation pipeline. The diagram illustrates the reverse diffusion process, where noisy latent variables ztz_{t} are iteratively denoised through a U-Net architecture. \n321\t\n322\t# 3.3 Direct Preference Optimization \n323\t\n324\tRecent advances in preference-based optimization have demonstrated the effectiveness of Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) for aligning large language models (LLMs). However, its application to cross-modal generation tasks, such as video-to-audio synthesis, remains underexplored. In this paper, We propose the first online-DPO framework for cross-modal preference alignment in video-conditioned audio generation. Unlike offline DPO that requires pre-collected static datasets, our method continuously adapts to human preferences through real-time interaction during the generation process.\n325\t\n326\t# 3.3.1 Theoretical Foundation\n327\t\n328\tDirect Preference Optimization (DPO) establishes a direct mapping between reward functions and optimal policies through three fundamental principles:\n329\t\n330\t- 1. \n331\t\n332\tOptimal Policy Characterization: For any reward function r⁡(v,a)r(v,a) and reference policy πref\\pi_{\\text{ref}}, the KL-constrained optimal policy has closed-form solution:\n333\t\n334\tπ∗​(a|v)=πref​(a|v)​exp⁡(r⁡(v,a)/β)Z⁡(v)\\pi^{*}(a|v)=\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a|v)\\exp(r(v,a)/\\beta)}{Z(v)}\n335\t\n336\t(15)\n337\t\n338\twhere Z⁡(v)=∑aπref​(a|v)​exp⁡(r⁡(v,a)/β)Z(v)=\\sum_{a}\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a|v)\\exp(r(v,a)/\\beta) is the partition function, vv and aa are the inputs and generated outputs. In our paper, vv represents the input video and aa represents the output audio. \n339\t\n340\t- 2. \n341\t\n342\tReward-Policy Duality: Equation 15 permits reward reparameterization via policy ratios:\n343\t\n344\tr⁡(v,a)=β​log⁡π∗​(a|v)πref​(a|v)+β​log⁡Z⁡(v)r(v,a)=\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi^{*}(a|v)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a|v)}+\\beta\\log Z(v)\n345\t\n346\t(16)\n347\t\n348\t- 3. \n349\t\n350\tPreference Modeling: Human judgments follow the Bradley-Terry model:\n351\t\n352\tp∗​(aw≻al|v)=exp⁡(r⁡(v,aw))exp⁡(r⁡(v,aw))+exp⁡(r⁡(v,al))p^{*}(a_{w}\\succ a_{l}|v)=\\frac{\\exp(r(v,a_{w}))}{\\exp(r(v,a_{w}))+\\exp(r(v,a_{l}))}\n353\t\n354\t(17)\n355\t\n356\twhere awa_{w} denotes the preferred output over ala_{l} for a given input vv. \n357\t\n358\tSubstituting 16 into 17 yields the DPO objective:\n359\t\n360\tℒDPO=−𝔼(v,aw,al)∼𝒟​[log⁡σ⁡(β​log⁡πθ​(aw|v)πref​(aw|v)−β​log⁡πθ​(al|v)πref​(al|v))]\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{DPO}}=-\\mathbb{E}_{(v,a_{w},a_{l})\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\left[\\log\\sigma\\left(\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{w}|v)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a_{w}|v)}-\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{l}|v)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a_{l}|v)}\\right)\\right]\n361\t\n362\t(18)\n363\t\n364\twhere σ⁡(⋅)\\sigma(\\cdot) denotes the sigmoid function, and 𝒟\\mathcal{D} is the preference dataset.\n365\t\n366\t# 3.3.2 Online-DPO\n367\t\n368\tWhile DPO provides an elegant framework for preference optimization, its conventional offline formulation faces fundamental scalability challenges. The approach critically depends on large-scale human-annotated preference data, which becomes prohibitively expensive to collect for complex cross-modal tasks like video-to-audio generation—where expert annotators must evaluate subtle audiovisual synchronization and acoustic quality. This dependency creates a bottleneck for adapting to new domains or evaluation criteria, as each requires fresh annotation campaigns. Moreover, the static nature of offline datasets leads to inevitable obsolescence: as generative models improve, human judgments collected on older model outputs become misaligned with current capabilities, causing the preference model to optimize for outdated behaviors. This problem is exacerbated in dynamic multi-modal settings where aesthetic standards and technical expectations evolve rapidly, rendering pre-collected preferences increasingly irrelevant over time.\n369\t\n370\tInspired by recent work 51, we propose an Online-DPO method which can explore an iterative self-training approach and uses no human-annotated preferences in the training loop. As illustrate in Figure 4, our onlin-DPO mainly consists of two parts: 1) areward modeling, and 2) iterative training using the modeled rewards.\n371\t\n372\tFigure 4: Pipeline of one Online-DPO process. We use a subset of VGGSound to fine-tune the whole model.\n373\t\n374\tReward Modeling\n375\tBuilding upon our motivation for preference-based optimization, we develop an automated reward modeling framework that addresses the key limitations of reconstruction-based training identified earlier. Recognizing that human preference annotation at scale is prohibitively expensive while raw audio-visual pairs may not reflect true perceptual quality, we propose a multi-dimensional reward system that captures the essential aspects of human evaluation. Our approach evaluates generated audio through three critical lenses that mirror human judgment criteria: (1) temporal and semantic alignment with visual content, (2) consistency with textual descriptions (when available), and (3) intrinsic acoustic quality. This tripartite evaluation directly addresses the ambiguity and subjectivity challenges highlighted in our introduction, where multiple audio outputs could technically match the video but vary significantly in perceptual quality.\n376\t\n377\tSpecifically, we assess the quality of the generated audio along three primary dimensions:\n378\t\n379\t- • \n380\t\n381\tAudio-Visual Correspondence: The temporal and contextual alignment between the synthesized audio and the input video content.\n382\t\n383\t- • \n384\t\n385\tAudio-Text Consistency: The accuracy with which the audio reflects accompanying textual descriptions.\n386\t\n387\t- • \n388\t\n389\tIntrinsic Audio Quality: The acoustic fidelity and clarity of the generated audio itself, irrespective of the conditioning inputs.\n390\t\n391\tTo operationalize this multi-faceted evaluation, we utilize specific automated metrics for each dimension. Audio-visual correspondence is quantified via the similarity score obtained from the pre-trained contrastive learning model CAV-MAE 15. Similarly, audio-text consistency is assessed using the similarity score derived from a dedicated audio-text contrastive learning model CLAP. Most crucially, we employ the recently proposed Audiobox-Aesthetics predictor from Meta 47. This predictor provides non-intrusive, utterance-level aesthetic scores based on human perceptual dimensions, trained across diverse audio types (speech, music, sounds), offering a robust measure of perceptual quality suitable for generative models.\n392\t\n393\tGiven that the three reward components—audio-visual correspondence similarity (Ra​vR_{av}), audio-text consistency similarity (Ra​tR_{at}), and the intrinsic audio quality score (Rq​u​a​l​i​t​yR_{quality}) from Audiobox-Aesthetics—operate on potentially different scales and represent distinct facets of audio generation quality, a direct summation is inadequate for creating a unified reward signal. To address this, we first normalize each component independently to ensure they contribute appropriately to the final reward. The final composite reward score R⁡(y)R(y) for a generated audio sample yy is then calculated as a weighted linear combination of these normalized scores:\n394\t\n395\tR⁡(y)=wa​v⋅Ra​v′​(y)+wa​t⋅Ra​t′​(y)+wq​u​a​l​i​t​y⋅Rq​u​a​l​i​t​y′​(y)R(y)=w_{av}\\cdot R^{\\prime}_{av}(y)+w_{at}\\cdot R^{\\prime}_{at}(y)+w_{quality}\\cdot R^{\\prime}_{quality}(y)\n396\t\n397\t(19)\n398\t\n399\tHere, wa​vw_{av}, wa​tw_{at}, and wq​u​a​l​i​t​yw_{quality} are non-negative hyperparameters representing the relative importance assigned to audio-visual correspondence, audio-text consistency, and intrinsic quality, respectively.\n400\t\n401\tIterative Training with Value Aware DPO Loss\n402\t\n403\tA key limitation of conventional offline DPO is its reliance on a static preference dataset. This dataset, collected prior to training, prevents the policy from receiving direct feedback on the quality variations within its own generated outputs during the alignment process. Consequently, the model may not efficiently learn to refine subtle aspects captured by the reward function, leading to suboptimal performance. To overcome this challenge, our Online-DPO framework implements an Online Preference Sample Generation strategy integrated within the iterative training loop.\n404\t\n405\tSpecifically, instead of drawing from a fixed dataset, preference pairs are generated dynamically in each training iteration tt. For a given input condition xx (video and text), the current policy πt\\pi_{t} generates a set of NN candidate audio samples, denoted as 𝒴={y1,y2,…,yN}\\mathcal{Y}=\\{y_{1},y_{2},...,y_{N}\\}. Each candidate yi∈𝒴y_{i}\\in\\mathcal{Y} is subsequently evaluated using our composite automated reward function R⁡(yi)R(y_{i})\n406\t\n407\tCrucially, we then identify the best-performing (ywy_{w}) and worst-performing (yly_{l}) samples within this generated set 𝒴\\mathcal{Y} based purely on the automated reward scores:\n408\t\n409\tyw=argmaxyi∈𝒴​R​(yi)andyl=argminyi∈𝒴​R​(yi)y_{w}=\\underset{y_{i}\\in\\mathcal{Y}}{\\operatorname{argmax}}R(y_{i})\\quad\\text{and}\\quad y_{l}=\\underset{y_{i}\\in\\mathcal{Y}}{\\operatorname{argmin}}R(y_{i})\n410\t\n411\t(20)\n412\t\n413\tThese dynamically generated preference pairs {x,yw,yl}\\{x,y_{w},y_{l}\\} form the online dataset 𝒟t\\mathcal{D}_{t} used to update the policy πθ\\pi_{\\theta}.\n414\t\n415\tTo further enhance learning efficiency and sensitivity to the magnitude of preference, we propose a Value-Aware DPO (VA-DPO) loss that explicitly incorporates reward magnitudes. Unlike standard DPO, which treats all preference pairs equally (as a binary choice), our loss accounts for the degree of preference between samples by integrating the reward margin. The resulting VA-DPO loss is defined as:\n416\t\n417\tℒVA-DPO(πθ;πref)=−𝔼(x,yw,yl)∼𝒟t[\\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{VA-DPO}}(\\pi_{\\theta};\\pi_{\\text{ref}})=-\\mathbb{E}_{\\begin{subarray}{c}(x,y_{w},y_{l})\\\\\n418\t\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{t}\\end{subarray}}\\bigg[\n419\tlog⁡σ⁡(β​log⁡πθ​(yw|x)πref​(yw|x)−β​log⁡πθ​(yl|x)πref​(yl|x)CLOSE\\displaystyle\\log\\sigma\\bigg(\\beta\\log\\tfrac{\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{w}|x)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(y_{w}|x)}-\\beta\\log\\tfrac{\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{l}|x)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(y_{l}|x)}\n420\t\n421\t(21)\n422\t\n423\t−λ(R(yw)−R(yl)))]\\displaystyle-\\lambda\\big(R(y_{w})-R(y_{l})\\big)\\bigg)\\bigg]\n424\t\n425\twhere σ⁡(⋅)\\sigma(\\cdot) is the sigmoid function, β\\beta is the temperature parameter controlling the weight of the policy difference, and λ\\lambda is a hyperparameter balancing the DPO term and the reward margin term. Considering that diffusion models are not like Large Language Models(LLMs), we follow Diffusion-DPO35; 49 for the loss calculation. Regarding the reference model πref\\pi_{\\text{ref}}, we update it periodically; specifically, at the end of each training epoch in our implementation.\n426\t\n427\t# 3.4 Test-time Scaling\n428\t\n429\tTo enhance the inference performance of our video-to-audio diffusion model, we propose a Dual-scale Diffusion Search (DDS) strategy. This approach introduces a dual-scale sampling mechanism that adaptively explores the latent space by combining both small and large step sizes, thereby balancing local exploitation and global exploration.\n430\t\n431\tThe DDS algorithm maintains a population of candidate solutions that evolve over multiple iterations. Initially, the population P0P_{0} is sampled from a standard normal distribution. At each iteration, for every candidate (generated audios) in the population, we generate two potential updates using different diffusion scales: a conservative step with parameter βs\\beta_{s} for local exploitation and a more aggressive step with parameter βl\\beta_{l} for global exploration (βl>βs)(\\beta_{l}>\\beta_{s}). Specifically, given a candidate solution, we generate two new candidates:\n432\t\n433\txs=βs​x+1−βs2⋅ηx_{s}=\\beta_{s}x+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{s}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n434\t\n435\t(22)\n436\t\n437\txl=βl​x+1−βl2⋅ηx_{l}=\\beta_{l}x+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{l}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n438\t\n439\t(23)\n440\t\n441\twhere η\\eta is sampled from 𝒩⁡(0,1)\\mathcal{N}(0,1).\n442\t\n443\tTo evaluate the quality of the generated audio, we employ a zero-shot evaluation approach. Specifically, we utilize the CLIP score 55 to measure the audio-visual correspondence and the CLAP score to assess the audio-text alignment. These metrics provide a comprehensive evaluation of the generated audio in terms of both visual and textual relevance. The complete DSS procedure is presented in Algorithm 1\n444\t\n445\tAlgorithm 1 Dual-scale Diffusion Search\n446\t\n447\t D,T,N,βs,βl,ℱD,T,N,\\beta_{s},\\beta_{l},\\mathcal{F}\n448\t\n449\t best_solution, best_score\n450\t\n451\t Initialize P0P_{0} with NN samples from 𝒩⁡(0,1)\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\n452\t\n453\t best_score⇐−∞\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow-\\infty\n454\t\n455\t best_solution⇐∅\\text{best\\_solution}\\Leftarrow\\emptyset\n456\t\n457\t for i⇐1i\\Leftarrow 1 to NN do\n458\t\n459\t   if ℱ​(P0​[i])>best_score\\mathcal{F}(P_{0}[i])>\\text{best\\_score} then\n460\t\n461\t    best_score⇐ℱ​(P0​[i])\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow\\mathcal{F}(P_{0}[i])\n462\t\n463\t    best_candidate⇐P0​[i]\\text{best\\_candidate}\\Leftarrow P_{0}[i]\n464\t\n465\t   end if\n466\t\n467\t end for\n468\t\n469\t for t⇐1t\\Leftarrow 1 to TT do\n470\t\n471\t   for i⇐1i\\Leftarrow 1 to NN do\n472\t\n473\t    Sample η\\eta from 𝒩​(0,1)D\\mathcal{N}(0,1)^{D}\n474\t\n475\t    xs⇐βs​Pt−1​[i]+1−βs2⋅ηx_{s}\\Leftarrow\\beta_{s}P_{t-1}[i]+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{s}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n476\t\n477\t    xl⇐βl​Pt−1​[i]+1−βl2⋅ηx_{l}\\Leftarrow\\beta_{l}P_{t-1}[i]+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{l}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n478\t\n479\t    if ℱ⁡(xs)>ℱ⁡(xl)\\mathcal{F}(x_{s})>\\mathcal{F}(x_{l}) then\n480\t\n481\t      Pt​[i]⇐xsP_{t}[i]\\Leftarrow x_{s}\n482\t\n483\t      if ℱ⁡(xs)>best_score\\mathcal{F}(x_{s})>\\text{best\\_score} then\n484\t\n485\t       best_score⇐ℱ⁡(xs)\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow\\mathcal{F}(x_{s})\n486\t\n487\t       best_candidate⇐xs\\text{best\\_candidate}\\Leftarrow x_{s}\n488\t\n489\t      end if\n490\t\n491\t    else\n492\t\n493\t      Pt​[i]⇐xlP_{t}[i]\\Leftarrow x_{l}\n494\t\n495\t      if ℱ⁡(xl)>best_score\\mathcal{F}(x_{l})>\\text{best\\_score} then\n496\t\n497\t       best_score⇐ℱ⁡(xl)\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow\\mathcal{F}(x_{l})\n498\t\n499\t       best_candidate⇐xl\\text{best\\_candidate}\\Leftarrow x_{l}\n500\t\n501\t      end if\n502\t\n503\t    end if\n504\t\n505\t   end for\n506\t\n507\t end for\n508\t\n509\t# 4 Experiments\n510\t\n511\t# Dataset\n512\t\n513\tTo train our proposed HarmoniDPO, we utilize the VGGSound 3 dataset, adhering to its original train/test splits. For evaluation, we assess our method using both the VGGSound and AVSync15 63 datasets.\n514\t\n515\t# Implementation Details\n516\t\n517\tWe use Tango-2 35, a pre-trained text-to-audio diffusion model as our based diffusion model. For visual feature extraction, we utilize OpenCLIP ViT-H/14 4 as our image encoder and ViCLIP-L-14 as our video encoder, where the latter is pre-trained on the InternVid-10M-FLT53 dataset. \n518\t\n519\tDuring training, We use 64 H800 GPUs for experiments. We employ the AdamW optimizer with a constant learning rate of 1×10−41\\times 10^{-4} and a batch size of 128 visual-audio embedding pairs. To enhance model generalization, we apply classifier-free guidance with a dropout rate of 0.05. The training process consists of two distinct phases: (1) In the visual conditioning phase, we keep the pre-trained diffusion model frozen while fine-tuning only the newly added projection head and cross-attention layers to adapt the text-to-audio diffusion model into a video-to-audio model; (2) In the subsequent alignment stage, we first sample 4500 videos independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) from the VGGSound dataset. For the online-DPO fine-tuning, we generate candidate audio samples for these videos. To ensure the generated candidates are of high quality and relevance, we condition the generation on the original text descriptions associated with each video. Furthermore, inspired by the methodology in Tango-2, we create diverse candidate sets by varying parameters such as the number of diffusion steps and the classifier-free guidance scale during generation. We set wa​vw_{av}, wa​tw_{at} and wq​u​a​l​i​t​yw_{quality} to be equal (1) for the reward modeling stage, adopting a balanced combination of audio-visual alignment, audio-text relevance, and perceptual quality scores. Finally, we perform end-to-end fine-tuning of the entire model using the online-DPO method, leveraging these generated candidates to learn preference alignments. This two-phase approach ensures stable training while effectively aligning the visual and audio modalities. \n520\t\n521\t# Metrics\n522\t\n523\tTo evaluate the performance, we employ a comprehensive set of metrics that assess various aspects of the generated audio, including: Mean KL Divergence (MKL)21 to measure the sample-level similarity between the generated audio and the ground truth; CLIP Similarity to evaluate the semantic coherence between the input video and the generated audio embeddings, using Wav2CLIP55 as the audio encoder and CLIP as the video encoder, as done in previous works 50; 65; Frechet Inception Distance (FID)18 and Frechet Audio Distance (FAD)26 with VGGish17 to assess the fidelity and distribution similarity of the generated audio; CLAP Similarity to evaluate the cross-modal alignment between text and generated audio; and Onset Acc (onset detection accuracy) and Onset AP (onset detection average precision) 57 to evaluate the generated audios, using the onset ground truth from the datasets.\n524\t\n525\t# Baseline\n526\t\n527\tFor comparison, we use current state-of-the-art method SpecVQGAN 21, Diff-Foley 33, V2A-Mapper 50, Seeing-and-hearing 58 and Foley-crafter 65. SpecVQGAN generates audio tokens autoregressively from video tokens, while Diff-Foley applies contrastive learning to achieve synchronized audio synthesis via its CAVP encoders. V2A-Mapper aligns image representations with audio embeddings in CLAP space, facilitating video-based audio generation through a pre-trained model. Seeing-and-hearing uses ImageBind 14 as a connector between visual and audio domains, enabling multimodal generation by integrating existing audio and video generators. FoleyCrafter introduces both semantic and temporal adapters to enhance video-to-audio generation.\n528\t\n529\t# 4.1 Qualitative Evaluation\n530\t\n531\tTable 1: Comparison of results on VGGSound dataset. The results show that our method can achieve better results with less data. The number underlined indicates the second best result.\n532\t\n533\tMethod\n534\tTraining Set\n535\tMKL ↓\\downarrow\n536\tCLIP ↑\\uparrow\n537\tFD↓\\downarrow\n538\tFAD ↓\\downarrow\n539\tCLAP ↑\\uparrow\n540\t\n541\tSpecVQGAN\n542\tVGGSound, VAS\n543\t3.40\n544\t5.88\n545\t32.01\n546\t5.79\n547\t-\n548\t\n549\tDiff-Foley\n550\tVGGSound, AudioSet-V2A\n551\t3.32\n552\t9.17\n553\t29.03\n554\t6.23\n555\t20.5\n556\t\n557\tV2A-Mapper\n558\tVGGSound, Land-scape\n559\t2.84\n560\t9.72\n561\t24.16\n562\t1.34\n563\t24.5\n564\t\n565\tFoleyCrafter\n566\tVGGSound, AudioSet Strong\n567\t2.56\n568\t10.70\n569\t19.67\n570\t2.78\n571\t25.3\n572\t\n573\tFieren\n574\tVGGSound\n575\t2.58\n576\t11.83\n577\t12.48\n578\t3.32\n579\t24.7\n580\t\n581\tHarmoniDPO(w/o alignment)\n582\tVGGSound\n583\t2.04\n584\t12.54\n585\t7.72\n586\t1.75\n587\t28.35\n588\t\n589\tHarmoniDPO (aligned)\n590\tVGGSound\n591\t1.86\n592\t13.27\n593\t6.54\n594\t1.64\n595\t31.75\n596\t\n597\tHarmoniDPO (aligned + DDS)\n598\tVGGSound\n599\t1.82\n600\t13.65\n601\t6.42\n602\t1.59\n603\t32.57\n604\t\n605\t# Audio quality and cross-modal alignment \n606\t\n607\tWe evaluate HarmoniDPO’s performance in audio generation quality and cross-modal alignment using the VGGSound dataset and AVSync-15 dataset, with quantitative results presented in Table 1 and Table 2. To ensure a fair comparison against baselines that only use visual input, we evaluate HarmoniDPO without leveraging text conditioning, thus avoiding any potential advantage from multimodal inputs.\n608\t\n609\tFor the VGGSound dataset, our evaluation specifically examines three progressively enhanced configurations of HarmoniDPO:\n610\t1) The base model utilizing pre-trained video and fine-grained frame embeddings without explicit alignment (HarmoniDPO (w/o alignment)).\n611\t2) The model enhanced with DPO-based alignment (HarmoniDPO (aligned)).\n612\t3) The DPO-aligned model further enhanced with Dual-scale Diffusion Search (DDS) during inference, where we set the number of search candidates N to 4 in our experiments.\n613\t\n614\tOur experimental results demonstrate significant performance improvements across these configurations. The base model already surpasses baseline methods by effectively leveraging pre-trained video embeddings and frame-level visual features. The DPO-aligned version shows marked improvements in audio-visual synchronization, particularly evident in CLIP and CLAP scores. The full model with DDS inference achieves optimal performance, maintaining competitive FAD scores while significantly outperforming all baselines in other metrics.\n615\t\n616\tTable 2: Comparison of results on AVSync15 dataset.\n617\t\n618\tMethod\n619\tTraining Set\n620\tMKL ↓\\downarrow\n621\tCLIP ↑\\uparrow\n622\tFID ↓\\downarrow\n623\t\n624\tSpecVQGAN\n625\tVGGSound, VAS\n626\t3.603\n627\t6.474\n628\t75.56\n629\t\n630\tDiff-Foley\n631\tVGGSound, AudioSet-V2A\n632\t1.963\n633\t10.38\n634\t65.77\n635\t\n636\tSeeing and Hearing\n637\tVGGSound, Land-scape\n638\t2.547\n639\t2.033\n640\t65.82\n641\t\n642\tFoleyCrafter\n643\tVGGSound, AudioSet Strong\n644\t1.479\n645\t11.94\n646\t36.80\n647\t\n648\tHarmoniDPO (Ours)\n649\tVGGSound\n650\t1.332\n651\t14.38\n652\t31.59\n653\t\n654\tFor the AVSync-15 dataset, we evaluate all methods on the test split, which consists of 150 distinct examples. The AVSync-15 dataset, curated from VGGSound’s most temporally challenging samples, serves as our benchmark for evaluating synchronization capabilities. We include a time alignment experiment in the subsequent analysis and limit our evaluation to three fundamental metrics. As shown in Table 2, our HarmoniDPO method achieves competitive or superior performance across all metrics. Specifically, HarmoniDPO excels in CLIP match scores and FID, highlighting its ability to generate audio-visual content that is both semantically aligned and visually coherent.\n655\t\n656\t# Time alignment\n657\t\n658\tTo evaluate temporal synchronization, we conduct experiments on the AVSync15 dataset. This dataset is constructed from high-quality videos sourced from VGGSound, which have been carefully filtered and segmented to retain only the most precise video-audio pairs while discarding irrelevant or inactive segments. As shown in Table 3, our method achieves state-of-the-art performance in temporal synchronization, demonstrating its effectiveness in aligning audio with visual events.\n659\t\n660\tTable 3: Comparison of results on AVSync15 dataset.\n661\t\n662\tMethod\n663\tOnset ACC ↑\\uparrow\n664\tOnset AP ↑\\uparrow\n665\t\n666\tSpecVQGAN\n667\t26.74\n668\t63.18\n669\t\n670\tDiff-Foley\n671\t21.18\n672\t66.55\n673\t\n674\tSeeing and Hearing\n675\t20.95\n676\t60.33\n677\t\n678\tFoleyCrafter\n679\t28.48\n680\t68.14\n681\t\n682\tHarmoniDPO (Ours)\n683\t32.53\n684\t69.97\n685\t\n686\t# 4.2 Online-DPO alignment\n687\t\n688\tTable 4: Comparison of results on VGGSound dataset. To enhance the robustness of the conclusions, we report the results without using DDS method.\n689\t\n690\tNum\n691\tMKL ↓\\downarrow\n692\tCLIP ↑\\uparrow\n693\tFID↓\\downarrow\n694\tFAD ↓\\downarrow\n695\tCLAP ↑\\uparrow\n696\t\n697\t2\n698\t1.97\n699\t12.33\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2145e040-dde2-4bb7-ba36-d7ae557878f2","uuid":"632cf942-8af4-4a6a-bccc-7cbe2ad68bd1","timestamp":"2026-08-13T11:37:52.077Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/paper_text.txt","content":"\nHowever, a fundamental limitation of conventional DPO approaches is their offline nature. While standard DPO can achieve RLHF-like effects, it remains an offline reinforcement learning method where the implicit “reward model” remains static throughout training. This limitation creates a critical distribution shift problem: as the policy model improves, it increasingly diverges from the preference model’s distribution, ultimately leading to performance bottlenecks. Our work addresses this limitation by introducing the first online DPO methodology for video-to-audio (V2A) generation. Our approach achieves the adaptive benefits of online reinforcement learning while preserving the computational efficiency of DPO. This innovation is particularly valuable in the V2A domain, where no prior alignment techniques have been applied, and where the complex, context-dependent nature of audio generation demands more dynamic preference modeling.\n\n# 3 Method\n\nIn this section, we begin by introducing the foundational concepts of LDMs and their applications in audio generation. We then present our proposed method, HarmoniDPO (HDPO), which integrates video, image to enable cross-modal audio generation with enhanced performance.\n\n# 3.1 Preliminaries\n\nLatent diffusion models (LDMs) 41 are a class of generative models built upon the principles of Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) 19. LDMs have emerged as powerful frameworks in text-to-audio and video-to-audio generation tasks. A key feature of LDMs is that they operate in a lower-dimensional latent space, making them computationally efficient. In the context of audio, raw signals are first transformed into mel-spectrograms. The mel-spectrogram, a time-frequency representation, logarithmically scales its frequency axis to the Mel scale, which mirrors human auditory perception, enabling the model to effectively learn both temporal dynamics and spectral characteristics.\n\nIn a video-to-audio system, given an audio signal xax_{a} and its corresponding video xvx_{v}, we first use a pre-trained variational autoencoder (VAE) with an encoder ℰθ\\mathcal{E}_{\\theta} to compress the mel-spectrogram representation of xax_{a} into a low-dimensional latent representation, denoted as z0=ℰθ​(xa)z_{0}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\theta}(x_{a}). This latent vector z0z_{0} is the target for the diffusion model.\n\nThe forward diffusion process gradually adds Gaussian noise to the latent vector z0z_{0} over a sequence of TT timesteps. This process is a fixed Markov chain defined as:\n\nq⁡(zt|zt−1)=𝒩⁡(zt,1−βt​zt−1,βt​𝐈)q(z_{t}|z_{t-1})=\\mathcal{N}(z_{t};\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{t}}z_{t-1},\\beta_{t}\\mathbf{I})\n\n(1)\n\nwhere {βt}t=1T\\{\\beta_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} is a pre-defined variance schedule. A useful property of this process is that we can sample ztz_{t} directly from z0z_{0} at any timestep tt:\n\nq⁡(zt|z0)=𝒩⁡(zt,α¯t​z0,(1−α¯t)​𝐈)q(z_{t}|z_{0})=\\mathcal{N}(z_{t};\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}z_{0},(1-\\bar{\\alpha}_{t})\\mathbf{I})\n\n(2)\n\nwhere α¯t=∏s=1t(1−βs)\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}=\\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\\beta_{s}). Using the reparameterization trick, any ztz_{t} can be expressed as zt=α¯t​z0+1−α¯t​ϵz_{t}=\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}z_{0}+\\sqrt{1-\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}\\epsilon, where ϵ∼𝒩⁡(0,𝐈)\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\mathbf{I}).\n\nThe reverse process aims to learn the true posterior q⁡(zt−1|zt)q(z_{t-1}|z_{t}), which would reverse the noising process. As this posterior is intractable, a neural network ϵθ\\epsilon_{\\theta} is trained to approximate it. As shown by Ho et al. 19, instead of modeling the reverse distribution’s mean directly, the training can be simplified to predicting the noise component ϵ\\epsilon that was added to create ztz_{t}. This leads to a simplified training objective, which is a mean-squared error loss between the true and predicted noise:\n\nℒLDM=𝔼z0,ϵ,t​[‖ϵ−ϵθ​(zt,t)‖22]\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{LDM}}=\\mathbb{E}_{z_{0},\\epsilon,t}\\left[\\|\\epsilon-\\epsilon_{\\theta}(z_{t},t)\\|_{2}^{2}\\right]\n\n(3)\n\nTo enable conditional generation, the denoising network ϵθ\\epsilon_{\\theta} is augmented with conditioning information, such as a visual embedding fvf_{v}, typically via a cross-attention mechanism. The objective thus becomes:\n\nℒLDM=𝔼z0,ϵ,t,fv​[‖ϵ−ϵθ​(zt,t,fv)‖22]\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{LDM}}=\\mathbb{E}_{z_{0},\\epsilon,t,f_{v}}\\left[\\|\\epsilon-\\epsilon_{\\theta}(z_{t},t,f_{v})\\|_{2}^{2}\\right]\n\n(4)\n\nDuring inference, given a visual embedding fvf_{v}, we can generate the corresponding audio by iterative denoising from random Gaussian noise zTz_{T}:\n\nzt−1=11−βt​(zt−βt1−α¯​t​ϵθ​(zt,t,fv))z_{t-1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{t}}}(z_{t}-\\frac{\\beta_{t}}{\\sqrt{1-\\bar{\\alpha}t}}\\epsilon_{\\theta}(z_{t},t,f_{v}))\n\n(5)\n\nGiven the limited size and varying quality of existing visual-audio datasets, we use a pre-trained text-to-audio diffusion model, Tango-2 35, as our base model, keeping it frozen throughout training. Figure 2 provides an overview of the framework, and we will describe each component of HarmoniDPO in detail in the following sections.\n\nFigure 2: Illustration of our HarmoniDPO model architecture. We propose a dual-feature representation framework that combines global video features with fine-grained frame-wise features.\n\n# 3.2 Visual Condition\n\nAccurate video representation is essential for effectively capturing the semantic content of videos. Previous methods, such as FoleyCrafter, have relied on the CLIP image encoder to obtain average semantic features from individual frames. However, since CLIP is not trained on video datasets, this approach may fall short of capturing the temporal dynamics and motion patterns inherent in video content. To address this limitation, we propose a dual-feature representation framework that combines global video features with fine-grained frame-wise features, enabling comprehensive encoding of both spatial and temporal information.\n\nAs shown in Figure  2, for global video feature extraction, we leverage the video encoder from InternVid 53, a state-of-the-art contrastive video-language pre-training framework. Given an input video VV containing TT frames, the global video feature fgf_{g} is computed as:\n\nfg=ℰInternVid​(V)∈ℝdgf_{g}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{InternVid}}(V)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{g}}\n\n(6)\n\nwhere ℰInternVid\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{InternVid}} represents the InternVid video encoder and dgd_{g} denotes the dimensionality of the extracted video feature.\nInternVid’s architecture extends CLIP’s visual understanding capabilities through continued pre-training on large-scale video-text datasets, enabling superior modeling of temporal relationships and motion dynamics across video sequences. The encoding process operates as follows: given a target video, we first establish a fixed frame sampling count MM. The video is then temporally subsampled at MM uniformly distributed timestamps to ensure consistent temporal coverage. The resulting frames are concatenated and processed through a pre-trained Vision Transformer to produce the final global video representation.\n\nWhile the global video features effectively capture high-level semantic content and temporal dynamics, fine-grained frame-level details remain crucial for maintaining precise temporal alignment between the generated audio and specific visual events. Therefore, we implement a complementary frame-level processing stream that operates parallel to global feature extraction. We uniformly sample NN frames {v1,v2,…,vN}\\{v_{1},v_{2},\\dots,v_{N}\\} from the input video and process each frame through the CLIP visual encoder to obtain frame-wise embeddings:\n\nei=ℰCLIP(vi)∈ℝdf,i∈1,2,…,Ne_{i}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{CLIP}}(v_{i})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{f}},\\quad i\\in{1,2,\\dots,N}\n\n(7)\n\nwhere ℰCLIP\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{CLIP}} represents the CLIP image encoder and dfd_{f} is the dimension of frame features. In our implementation, we sample N=250N=250 frames from the original video as uniformly as possible. For videos containing fewer than 250 frames, we pad the sequence with zero-filled frame features. Note that the majority of samples in our training dataset exceed 250 frames, making padding necessary for only a small portion of the data.\n\nFor effective multimodal fusion, we first use projection layers to align the dimensions of global and frame-level features to a common dimension dcd_{c}:\n\nfg′\\displaystyle f^{\\prime}_{g}\n=Wg​fg∈ℝdc\\displaystyle=W_{g}f_{g}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}\n\n(8)\n\nei′\\displaystyle e^{\\prime}_{i}\n=Wf​ei∈ℝdc\\displaystyle=W_{f}e_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}\n\n(9)\n\nwhere Wg∈ℝdc×dgW_{g}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}\\times d_{g}} and Wf∈ℝdc×dfW_{f}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}\\times d_{f}} are learnable projection matrices. We then concatenate the projected global video feature with the projected frame-wise embeddings:\n\nF=[fg′;e1′;e2′;…;eN′]∈ℝ(1+N)×dcF=[f^{\\prime}_{g};e^{\\prime}_{1};e^{\\prime}_{2};\\dots;e^{\\prime}_{N}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{c}}\n\n(10)\n\nFinally, we employ another projection layer to transform the concatenated features to the model’s hidden dimension:\n\nF′=Wh​F∈ℝ(1+N)×dhF^{\\prime}=W_{h}F\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}}\n\n(11)\n\nwhere Wh∈ℝdh×dcW_{h}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\\times d_{c}} is the final projection matrix and dhd_{h} is the hidden dimension of the model.\n\nTo better capture temporal relationships among the fused features, we incorporate Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) 43 within a multi-head self-attention mechanism. The self-attention operation employs separate learned projections for queries, keys, and values:\n\nQ=F′​Wq∈ℝ(1+N)×dh,K=F′​Wk∈ℝ(1+N)×dh,V=F′​Wv∈ℝ(1+N)×dh\\displaystyle Q=F^{\\prime}W_{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}},\\ K=F^{\\prime}W_{k}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}},\\ V=F^{\\prime}W_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}}\n\n(12)\n\nwhere Wq,Wk,Wv∈ℝdh×dhW_{q},W_{k},W_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\\times d_{h}} are learnable projection matrices for queries, keys, and values, respectively. The final fused representation is obtained by:\n\nFfused=AttentionRoPE​(Q,K,V)∈ℝ(1+N)×dhF_{\\text{fused}}=\\text{Attention}_{\\text{RoPE}}(Q,K,V)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+N)\\times d_{h}}\n\n(13)\n\nwhere RoPE is applied to both queries QQ and keys KK before computing attention scores, enabling the model to encode sequential ordering of frames through relative position information. These fused representations are subsequently incorporated into the diffusion model via a cross-attention layer (visual-attention), enabling the denoising process to dynamically attend to spatio-temporal features during training. \n\nBuilding upon the comprehensive visual, our method integrates these multimodal conditions into a diffusion-based audio generation framework. As illustrated in Figure  3, the generation process operates within the latent space of a pre-trained Variational Autoencoder (VAE) for computational efficiency. The reverse diffusion process begins with noisy latent variables zTz_{T} and iteratively denoises them through a U-Net architecture, where the fused visual representations FfusedF_{\\text{fused}} are injected via cross-attention layers to guide the denoising process. The final denoised latent z0z_{0} is then decoded through the VAE decoder to generate the target audio output.\n\nOur model also supports the optional textual conditioning for enhanced control over the generation process. For the Text input, we use the pre-trained FLAN-T56 text encoder to extract the text embedding ftf_{t}. Given a text prompt TT, the text embedding ftf_{t} is computed as:\n\nft=ℰT5text​(T)∈ℝdtf_{t}=\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{T5}}^{\\text{text}}(T)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}}\n\n(14)\n\nwhere ℰT5text\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{T5}}^{\\text{text}} denotes the FLAN-T5 text encoder and dtd_{t} is the dimension of the text feature. We use a projection layer to match the hidden dimension. The diffusion model then integrates these textual conditions through the cross-attention layer.\n\nFigure 3: Overview of the video-conditioned audio generation pipeline. The diagram illustrates the reverse diffusion process, where noisy latent variables ztz_{t} are iteratively denoised through a U-Net architecture. \n\n# 3.3 Direct Preference Optimization \n\nRecent advances in preference-based optimization have demonstrated the effectiveness of Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) for aligning large language models (LLMs). However, its application to cross-modal generation tasks, such as video-to-audio synthesis, remains underexplored. In this paper, We propose the first online-DPO framework for cross-modal preference alignment in video-conditioned audio generation. Unlike offline DPO that requires pre-collected static datasets, our method continuously adapts to human preferences through real-time interaction during the generation process.\n\n# 3.3.1 Theoretical Foundation\n\nDirect Preference Optimization (DPO) establishes a direct mapping between reward functions and optimal policies through three fundamental principles:\n\n- 1. \n\nOptimal Policy Characterization: For any reward function r⁡(v,a)r(v,a) and reference policy πref\\pi_{\\text{ref}}, the KL-constrained optimal policy has closed-form solution:\n\nπ∗​(a|v)=πref​(a|v)​exp⁡(r⁡(v,a)/β)Z⁡(v)\\pi^{*}(a|v)=\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a|v)\\exp(r(v,a)/\\beta)}{Z(v)}\n\n(15)\n\nwhere Z⁡(v)=∑aπref​(a|v)​exp⁡(r⁡(v,a)/β)Z(v)=\\sum_{a}\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a|v)\\exp(r(v,a)/\\beta) is the partition function, vv and aa are the inputs and generated outputs. In our paper, vv represents the input video and aa represents the output audio. \n\n- 2. \n\nReward-Policy Duality: Equation 15 permits reward reparameterization via policy ratios:\n\nr⁡(v,a)=β​log⁡π∗​(a|v)πref​(a|v)+β​log⁡Z⁡(v)r(v,a)=\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi^{*}(a|v)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a|v)}+\\beta\\log Z(v)\n\n(16)\n\n- 3. \n\nPreference Modeling: Human judgments follow the Bradley-Terry model:\n\np∗​(aw≻al|v)=exp⁡(r⁡(v,aw))exp⁡(r⁡(v,aw))+exp⁡(r⁡(v,al))p^{*}(a_{w}\\succ a_{l}|v)=\\frac{\\exp(r(v,a_{w}))}{\\exp(r(v,a_{w}))+\\exp(r(v,a_{l}))}\n\n(17)\n\nwhere awa_{w} denotes the preferred output over ala_{l} for a given input vv. \n\nSubstituting 16 into 17 yields the DPO objective:\n\nℒDPO=−𝔼(v,aw,al)∼𝒟​[log⁡σ⁡(β​log⁡πθ​(aw|v)πref​(aw|v)−β​log⁡πθ​(al|v)πref​(al|v))]\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{DPO}}=-\\mathbb{E}_{(v,a_{w},a_{l})\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\left[\\log\\sigma\\left(\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{w}|v)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a_{w}|v)}-\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{l}|v)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(a_{l}|v)}\\right)\\right]\n\n(18)\n\nwhere σ⁡(⋅)\\sigma(\\cdot) denotes the sigmoid function, and 𝒟\\mathcal{D} is the preference dataset.\n\n# 3.3.2 Online-DPO\n\nWhile DPO provides an elegant framework for preference optimization, its conventional offline formulation faces fundamental scalability challenges. The approach critically depends on large-scale human-annotated preference data, which becomes prohibitively expensive to collect for complex cross-modal tasks like video-to-audio generation—where expert annotators must evaluate subtle audiovisual synchronization and acoustic quality. This dependency creates a bottleneck for adapting to new domains or evaluation criteria, as each requires fresh annotation campaigns. Moreover, the static nature of offline datasets leads to inevitable obsolescence: as generative models improve, human judgments collected on older model outputs become misaligned with current capabilities, causing the preference model to optimize for outdated behaviors. This problem is exacerbated in dynamic multi-modal settings where aesthetic standards and technical expectations evolve rapidly, rendering pre-collected preferences increasingly irrelevant over time.\n\nInspired by recent work 51, we propose an Online-DPO method which can explore an iterative self-training approach and uses no human-annotated preferences in the training loop. As illustrate in Figure 4, our onlin-DPO mainly consists of two parts: 1) areward modeling, and 2) iterative training using the modeled rewards.\n\nFigure 4: Pipeline of one Online-DPO process. We use a subset of VGGSound to fine-tune the whole model.\n\nReward Modeling\nBuilding upon our motivation for preference-based optimization, we develop an automated reward modeling framework that addresses the key limitations of reconstruction-based training identified earlier. Recognizing that human preference annotation at scale is prohibitively expensive while raw audio-visual pairs may not reflect true perceptual quality, we propose a multi-dimensional reward system that captures the essential aspects of human evaluation. Our approach evaluates generated audio through three critical lenses that mirror human judgment criteria: (1) temporal and semantic alignment with visual content, (2) consistency with textual descriptions (when available), and (3) intrinsic acoustic quality. This tripartite evaluation directly addresses the ambiguity and subjectivity challenges highlighted in our introduction, where multiple audio outputs could technically match the video but vary significantly in perceptual quality.\n\nSpecifically, we assess the quality of the generated audio along three primary dimensions:\n\n- • \n\nAudio-Visual Correspondence: The temporal and contextual alignment between the synthesized audio and the input video content.\n\n- • \n\nAudio-Text Consistency: The accuracy with which the audio reflects accompanying textual descriptions.\n\n- • \n\nIntrinsic Audio Quality: The acoustic fidelity and clarity of the generated audio itself, irrespective of the conditioning inputs.\n\nTo operationalize this multi-faceted evaluation, we utilize specific automated metrics for each dimension. Audio-visual correspondence is quantified via the similarity score obtained from the pre-trained contrastive learning model CAV-MAE 15. Similarly, audio-text consistency is assessed using the similarity score derived from a dedicated audio-text contrastive learning model CLAP. Most crucially, we employ the recently proposed Audiobox-Aesthetics predictor from Meta 47. This predictor provides non-intrusive, utterance-level aesthetic scores based on human perceptual dimensions, trained across diverse audio types (speech, music, sounds), offering a robust measure of perceptual quality suitable for generative models.\n\nGiven that the three reward components—audio-visual correspondence similarity (Ra​vR_{av}), audio-text consistency similarity (Ra​tR_{at}), and the intrinsic audio quality score (Rq​u​a​l​i​t​yR_{quality}) from Audiobox-Aesthetics—operate on potentially different scales and represent distinct facets of audio generation quality, a direct summation is inadequate for creating a unified reward signal. To address this, we first normalize each component independently to ensure they contribute appropriately to the final reward. The final composite reward score R⁡(y)R(y) for a generated audio sample yy is then calculated as a weighted linear combination of these normalized scores:\n\nR⁡(y)=wa​v⋅Ra​v′​(y)+wa​t⋅Ra​t′​(y)+wq​u​a​l​i​t​y⋅Rq​u​a​l​i​t​y′​(y)R(y)=w_{av}\\cdot R^{\\prime}_{av}(y)+w_{at}\\cdot R^{\\prime}_{at}(y)+w_{quality}\\cdot R^{\\prime}_{quality}(y)\n\n(19)\n\nHere, wa​vw_{av}, wa​tw_{at}, and wq​u​a​l​i​t​yw_{quality} are non-negative hyperparameters representing the relative importance assigned to audio-visual correspondence, audio-text consistency, and intrinsic quality, respectively.\n\nIterative Training with Value Aware DPO Loss\n\nA key limitation of conventional offline DPO is its reliance on a static preference dataset. This dataset, collected prior to training, prevents the policy from receiving direct feedback on the quality variations within its own generated outputs during the alignment process. Consequently, the model may not efficiently learn to refine subtle aspects captured by the reward function, leading to suboptimal performance. To overcome this challenge, our Online-DPO framework implements an Online Preference Sample Generation strategy integrated within the iterative training loop.\n\nSpecifically, instead of drawing from a fixed dataset, preference pairs are generated dynamically in each training iteration tt. For a given input condition xx (video and text), the current policy πt\\pi_{t} generates a set of NN candidate audio samples, denoted as 𝒴={y1,y2,…,yN}\\mathcal{Y}=\\{y_{1},y_{2},...,y_{N}\\}. Each candidate yi∈𝒴y_{i}\\in\\mathcal{Y} is subsequently evaluated using our composite automated reward function R⁡(yi)R(y_{i})\n\nCrucially, we then identify the best-performing (ywy_{w}) and worst-performing (yly_{l}) samples within this generated set 𝒴\\mathcal{Y} based purely on the automated reward scores:\n\nyw=argmaxyi∈𝒴​R​(yi)andyl=argminyi∈𝒴​R​(yi)y_{w}=\\underset{y_{i}\\in\\mathcal{Y}}{\\operatorname{argmax}}R(y_{i})\\quad\\text{and}\\quad y_{l}=\\underset{y_{i}\\in\\mathcal{Y}}{\\operatorname{argmin}}R(y_{i})\n\n(20)\n\nThese dynamically generated preference pairs {x,yw,yl}\\{x,y_{w},y_{l}\\} form the online dataset 𝒟t\\mathcal{D}_{t} used to update the policy πθ\\pi_{\\theta}.\n\nTo further enhance learning efficiency and sensitivity to the magnitude of preference, we propose a Value-Aware DPO (VA-DPO) loss that explicitly incorporates reward magnitudes. Unlike standard DPO, which treats all preference pairs equally (as a binary choice), our loss accounts for the degree of preference between samples by integrating the reward margin. The resulting VA-DPO loss is defined as:\n\nℒVA-DPO(πθ;πref)=−𝔼(x,yw,yl)∼𝒟t[\\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{VA-DPO}}(\\pi_{\\theta};\\pi_{\\text{ref}})=-\\mathbb{E}_{\\begin{subarray}{c}(x,y_{w},y_{l})\\\\\n\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{t}\\end{subarray}}\\bigg[\nlog⁡σ⁡(β​log⁡πθ​(yw|x)πref​(yw|x)−β​log⁡πθ​(yl|x)πref​(yl|x)CLOSE\\displaystyle\\log\\sigma\\bigg(\\beta\\log\\tfrac{\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{w}|x)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(y_{w}|x)}-\\beta\\log\\tfrac{\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{l}|x)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(y_{l}|x)}\n\n(21)\n\n−λ(R(yw)−R(yl)))]\\displaystyle-\\lambda\\big(R(y_{w})-R(y_{l})\\big)\\bigg)\\bigg]\n\nwhere σ⁡(⋅)\\sigma(\\cdot) is the sigmoid function, β\\beta is the temperature parameter controlling the weight of the policy difference, and λ\\lambda is a hyperparameter balancing the DPO term and the reward margin term. Considering that diffusion models are not like Large Language Models(LLMs), we follow Diffusion-DPO35; 49 for the loss calculation. Regarding the reference model πref\\pi_{\\text{ref}}, we update it periodically; specifically, at the end of each training epoch in our implementation.\n\n# 3.4 Test-time Scaling\n\nTo enhance the inference performance of our video-to-audio diffusion model, we propose a Dual-scale Diffusion Search (DDS) strategy. This approach introduces a dual-scale sampling mechanism that adaptively explores the latent space by combining both small and large step sizes, thereby balancing local exploitation and global exploration.\n\nThe DDS algorithm maintains a population of candidate solutions that evolve over multiple iterations. Initially, the population P0P_{0} is sampled from a standard normal distribution. At each iteration, for every candidate (generated audios) in the population, we generate two potential updates using different diffusion scales: a conservative step with parameter βs\\beta_{s} for local exploitation and a more aggressive step with parameter βl\\beta_{l} for global exploration (βl>βs)(\\beta_{l}>\\beta_{s}). Specifically, given a candidate solution, we generate two new candidates:\n\nxs=βs​x+1−βs2⋅ηx_{s}=\\beta_{s}x+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{s}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n\n(22)\n\nxl=βl​x+1−βl2⋅ηx_{l}=\\beta_{l}x+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{l}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n\n(23)\n\nwhere η\\eta is sampled from 𝒩⁡(0,1)\\mathcal{N}(0,1).\n\nTo evaluate the quality of the generated audio, we employ a zero-shot evaluation approach. Specifically, we utilize the CLIP score 55 to measure the audio-visual correspondence and the CLAP score to assess the audio-text alignment. These metrics provide a comprehensive evaluation of the generated audio in terms of both visual and textual relevance. The complete DSS procedure is presented in Algorithm 1\n\nAlgorithm 1 Dual-scale Diffusion Search\n\n D,T,N,βs,βl,ℱD,T,N,\\beta_{s},\\beta_{l},\\mathcal{F}\n\n best_solution, best_score\n\n Initialize P0P_{0} with NN samples from 𝒩⁡(0,1)\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\n\n best_score⇐−∞\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow-\\infty\n\n best_solution⇐∅\\text{best\\_solution}\\Leftarrow\\emptyset\n\n for i⇐1i\\Leftarrow 1 to NN do\n\n   if ℱ​(P0​[i])>best_score\\mathcal{F}(P_{0}[i])>\\text{best\\_score} then\n\n    best_score⇐ℱ​(P0​[i])\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow\\mathcal{F}(P_{0}[i])\n\n    best_candidate⇐P0​[i]\\text{best\\_candidate}\\Leftarrow P_{0}[i]\n\n   end if\n\n end for\n\n for t⇐1t\\Leftarrow 1 to TT do\n\n   for i⇐1i\\Leftarrow 1 to NN do\n\n    Sample η\\eta from 𝒩​(0,1)D\\mathcal{N}(0,1)^{D}\n\n    xs⇐βs​Pt−1​[i]+1−βs2⋅ηx_{s}\\Leftarrow\\beta_{s}P_{t-1}[i]+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{s}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n\n    xl⇐βl​Pt−1​[i]+1−βl2⋅ηx_{l}\\Leftarrow\\beta_{l}P_{t-1}[i]+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_{l}^{2}}\\cdot\\eta\n\n    if ℱ⁡(xs)>ℱ⁡(xl)\\mathcal{F}(x_{s})>\\mathcal{F}(x_{l}) then\n\n      Pt​[i]⇐xsP_{t}[i]\\Leftarrow x_{s}\n\n      if ℱ⁡(xs)>best_score\\mathcal{F}(x_{s})>\\text{best\\_score} then\n\n       best_score⇐ℱ⁡(xs)\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow\\mathcal{F}(x_{s})\n\n       best_candidate⇐xs\\text{best\\_candidate}\\Leftarrow x_{s}\n\n      end if\n\n    else\n\n      Pt​[i]⇐xlP_{t}[i]\\Leftarrow x_{l}\n\n      if ℱ⁡(xl)>best_score\\mathcal{F}(x_{l})>\\text{best\\_score} then\n\n       best_score⇐ℱ⁡(xl)\\text{best\\_score}\\Leftarrow\\mathcal{F}(x_{l})\n\n       best_candidate⇐xl\\text{best\\_candidate}\\Leftarrow x_{l}\n\n      end if\n\n    end if\n\n   end for\n\n end for\n\n# 4 Experiments\n\n# Dataset\n\nTo train our proposed HarmoniDPO, we utilize the VGGSound 3 dataset, adhering to its original train/test splits. For evaluation, we assess our method using both the VGGSound and AVSync15 63 datasets.\n\n# Implementation Details\n\nWe use Tango-2 35, a pre-trained text-to-audio diffusion model as our based diffusion model. For visual feature extraction, we utilize OpenCLIP ViT-H/14 4 as our image encoder and ViCLIP-L-14 as our video encoder, where the latter is pre-trained on the InternVid-10M-FLT53 dataset. \n\nDuring training, We use 64 H800 GPUs for experiments. We employ the AdamW optimizer with a constant learning rate of 1×10−41\\times 10^{-4} and a batch size of 128 visual-audio embedding pairs. To enhance model generalization, we apply classifier-free guidance with a dropout rate of 0.05. The training process consists of two distinct phases: (1) In the visual conditioning phase, we keep the pre-trained diffusion model frozen while fine-tuning only the newly added projection head and cross-attention layers to adapt the text-to-audio diffusion model into a video-to-audio model; (2) In the subsequent alignment stage, we first sample 4500 videos independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) from the VGGSound dataset. For the online-DPO fine-tuning, we generate candidate audio samples for these videos. To ensure the generated candidates are of high quality and relevance, we condition the generation on the original text descriptions associated with each video. Furthermore, inspired by the methodology in Tango-2, we create diverse candidate sets by varying parameters such as the number of diffusion steps and the classifier-free guidance scale during generation. We set wa​vw_{av}, wa​tw_{at} and wq​u​a​l​i​t​yw_{quality} to be equal (1) for the reward modeling stage, adopting a balanced combination of audio-visual alignment, audio-text relevance, and perceptual quality scores. Finally, we perform end-to-end fine-tuning of the entire model using the online-DPO method, leveraging these generated candidates to learn preference alignments. This two-phase approach ensures stable training while effectively aligning the visual and audio modalities. \n\n# Metrics\n\nTo evaluate the performance, we employ a comprehensive set of metrics that assess various aspects of the generated audio, including: Mean KL Divergence (MKL)21 to measure the sample-level similarity between the generated audio and the ground truth; CLIP Similarity to evaluate the semantic coherence between the input video and the generated audio embeddings, using Wav2CLIP55 as the audio encoder and CLIP as the video encoder, as done in previous works 50; 65; Frechet Inception Distance (FID)18 and Frechet Audio Distance (FAD)26 with VGGish17 to assess the fidelity and distribution similarity of the generated audio; CLAP Similarity to evaluate the cross-modal alignment between text and generated audio; and Onset Acc (onset detection accuracy) and Onset AP (onset detection average precision) 57 to evaluate the generated audios, using the onset ground truth from the datasets.\n\n# Baseline\n\nFor comparison, we use current state-of-the-art method SpecVQGAN 21, Diff-Foley 33, V2A-Mapper 50, Seeing-and-hearing 58 and Foley-crafter 65. SpecVQGAN generates audio tokens autoregressively from video tokens, while Diff-Foley applies contrastive learning to achieve synchronized audio synthesis via its CAVP encoders. V2A-Mapper aligns image representations with audio embeddings in CLAP space, facilitating video-based audio generation through a pre-trained model. Seeing-and-hearing uses ImageBind 14 as a connector between visual and audio domains, enabling multimodal generation by integrating existing audio and video generators. FoleyCrafter introduces both semantic and temporal adapters to enhance video-to-audio generation.\n\n# 4.1 Qualitative Evaluation\n\nTable 1: Comparison of results on VGGSound dataset. The results show that our method can achieve better results with less data. The number underlined indicates the second best result.\n\nMethod\nTraining Set\nMKL ↓\\downarrow\nCLIP ↑\\uparrow\nFD↓\\downarrow\nFAD ↓\\downarrow\nCLAP ↑\\uparrow\n\nSpecVQGAN\nVGGSound, VAS\n3.40\n5.88\n32.01\n5.79\n-\n\nDiff-Foley\nVGGSound, AudioSet-V2A\n3.32\n9.17\n29.03\n6.23\n20.5\n\nV2A-Mapper\nVGGSound, Land-scape\n2.84\n9.72\n24.16\n1.34\n24.5\n\nFoleyCrafter\nVGGSound, AudioSet Strong\n2.56\n10.70\n19.67\n2.78\n25.3\n\nFieren\nVGGSound\n2.58\n11.83\n12.48\n3.32\n24.7\n\nHarmoniDPO(w/o alignment)\nVGGSound\n2.04\n12.54\n7.72\n1.75\n28.35\n\nHarmoniDPO (aligned)\nVGGSound\n1.86\n13.27\n6.54\n1.64\n31.75\n\nHarmoniDPO (aligned + DDS)\nVGGSound\n1.82\n13.65\n6.42\n1.59\n32.57\n\n# Audio quality and cross-modal alignment \n\nWe evaluate HarmoniDPO’s performance in audio generation quality and cross-modal alignment using the VGGSound dataset and AVSync-15 dataset, with quantitative results presented in Table 1 and Table 2. To ensure a fair comparison against baselines that only use visual input, we evaluate HarmoniDPO without leveraging text conditioning, thus avoiding any potential advantage from multimodal inputs.\n\nFor the VGGSound dataset, our evaluation specifically examines three progressively enhanced configurations of HarmoniDPO:\n1) The base model utilizing pre-trained video and fine-grained frame embeddings without explicit alignment (HarmoniDPO (w/o alignment)).\n2) The model enhanced with DPO-based alignment (HarmoniDPO (aligned)).\n3) The DPO-aligned model further enhanced with Dual-scale Diffusion Search (DDS) during inference, where we set the number of search candidates N to 4 in our experiments.\n\nOur experimental results demonstrate significant performance improvements across these configurations. The base model already surpasses baseline methods by effectively leveraging pre-trained video embeddings and frame-level visual features. The DPO-aligned version shows marked improvements in audio-visual synchronization, particularly evident in CLIP and CLAP scores. The full model with DDS inference achieves optimal performance, maintaining competitive FAD scores while significantly outperforming all baselines in other metrics.\n\nTable 2: Comparison of results on AVSync15 dataset.\n\nMethod\nTraining Set\nMKL ↓\\downarrow\nCLIP ↑\\uparrow\nFID ↓\\downarrow\n\nSpecVQGAN\nVGGSound, VAS\n3.603\n6.474\n75.56\n\nDiff-Foley\nVGGSound, AudioSet-V2A\n1.963\n10.38\n65.77\n\nSeeing and Hearing\nVGGSound, Land-scape\n2.547\n2.033\n65.82\n\nFoleyCrafter\nVGGSound, AudioSet Strong\n1.479\n11.94\n36.80\n\nHarmoniDPO (Ours)\nVGGSound\n1.332\n14.38\n31.59\n\nFor the AVSync-15 dataset, we evaluate all methods on the test split, which consists of 150 distinct examples. The AVSync-15 dataset, curated from VGGSound’s most temporally challenging samples, serves as our benchmark for evaluating synchronization capabilities. We include a time alignment experiment in the subsequent analysis and limit our evaluation to three fundamental metrics. As shown in Table 2, our HarmoniDPO method achieves competitive or superior performance across all metrics. Specifically, HarmoniDPO excels in CLIP match scores and FID, highlighting its ability to generate audio-visual content that is both semantically aligned and visually coherent.\n\n# Time alignment\n\nTo evaluate temporal synchronization, we conduct experiments on the AVSync15 dataset. This dataset is constructed from high-quality videos sourced from VGGSound, which have been carefully filtered and segmented to retain only the most precise video-audio pairs while discarding irrelevant or inactive segments. As shown in Table 3, our method achieves state-of-the-art performance in temporal synchronization, demonstrating its effectiveness in aligning audio with visual events.\n\nTable 3: Comparison of results on AVSync15 dataset.\n\nMethod\nOnset ACC ↑\\uparrow\nOnset AP ↑\\uparrow\n\nSpecVQGAN\n26.74\n63.18\n\nDiff-Foley\n21.18\n66.55\n\nSeeing and Hearing\n20.95\n60.33\n\nFoleyCrafter\n28.48\n68.14\n\nHarmoniDPO (Ours)\n32.53\n69.97\n\n# 4.2 Online-DPO alignment\n\nTable 4: Comparison of results on VGGSound dataset. To enhance the robustness of the conclusions, we report the results without using DDS method.\n\nNum\nMKL ↓\\downarrow\nCLIP ↑\\uparrow\nFID↓\\downarrow\nFAD ↓\\downarrow\nCLAP ↑\\uparrow\n\n2\n1.97\n12.33","numLines":500,"startLine":200,"totalLines":1556}}}
